 "action_summary": "The owner asks why the system cannot be shown an advanced coding action once and then keep it for good. After the previous change a change shown once is kept in a lesson file and repeated, but a use nobody showed is refused: measured on the held-out commits of other projects, that refusal prevented no wrong edit and cost 18 of 59 right ones. This change lets the project's own check settle what the examples do not: beside the proven rules the lesson is read more widely (each part the change merely copied, when it starts from a variable, may be any other code), the places only that reading fits and the uses nobody showed are tried one at a time, and a try is kept only when two runs agree: no test that passed on the file as it is fails with the place changed, and a test that passes with it fails when that place is made to fail. A check that passes either way settles nothing and the place stays as it was. The check's answer is written back into the lesson as a new example, with the unsettled places named, so the next file needs no try. One new action on the same program, taught as one sentence through the existing compiler; no registry row, no change to the front door, no governance change.",
